Browse all foot care on our shop →

Frequently Asked Questions

Do not rely on a generalized belt rule. If your registration page requires a belt or another reflective item, missing it means you do not meet the published list. Recheck the official page and class emails before travel, then lay out every required item the night before.

Check your specific event's official packing list. The official page used for this update listed 30 lb at or above 150 lb bodyweight and 20 lb below 150 lb, but your event page always overrides this guide.

This is why many participants pack a backup water bottle. Any bladder can fail, and a Nalgene or collapsible HydraPak in a side pocket means you can still drink if your primary system fails partway through.

Both lights on this page can run on AAA batteries: the Petzl Actik Core takes its rechargeable CORE battery or three AAAs, and the Black Diamond Spot 400 takes three AAAs or Black Diamond's rechargeable pack. Charge or replace before the event, and a spare set of AAAs in your dry bag is a light backup.

There is no fixed mileage, but a good sign is several long training rucks at event load without hot spots or blisters. New boots the week before an event make blisters far more likely, whatever the brand.

This varies by retailer and item, so check each return policy before you buy. Don't count on returning used training gear; buy what you will use beyond the single event.

Do not invent a contribution from a generic list. Bring a team or class item only when your event page, class communication, or cadre instructions call for it.

